Evidence rules:
- Resolve the page-opening table and governing heading from prior source
  context before judging its scope. The nearest preceding printed table title
  takes precedence over older titles and detector-generated names. Eligible
  detail rows before the first current-page heading still belong to that
  preceding block; do not discard them because its heading is on another page.
- Establish source-block eligibility from the array description before
  enumerating rows. Similar columns do not make an unrelated source table
  eligible. Match the printed table's scope, geography, subject, and level of
  detail. Do not use a detector-generated name as a printed source title.
- Apply the schema's row exclusions before data-row enumeration. Excluded
  geography headings, group totals, and summary rows may have numeric cells;
  those cells do not make them eligible detail rows. An out-of-scope table may
  still supply explicitly requested document_fields, but must emit no array rows.
- Follow every schema and field description literally. Use the page image to
  align labels and value columns, and use the prepared page text above to
  transcribe exact text.
- For every value, transcribe the source first and then apply only the
  transformations required by that field's description. Preserve every source
  character or delimiter that the description does not explicitly remove or
  reformat. Never borrow a transformation from another field.
- Among eligible detail rows, build rows before assigning headings. Enumerate
  every visual row with a value in at least one data column named by the
  selected array schema, and emit that row exactly once. A bold, indented, or
  subgroup label with data is still the
  subject of a data row.
- Reconcile that enumeration against the supplied page-table row inventory.
  Every inventoried source line with a value in a schema data column must map to
  one emitted row only when it satisfies that array's scope and row rules.
  Ignore inventoried headers, excluded aggregate headings, section-only rows,
  separators, and blanks rather than treating every inventoried line as data.
- For a field explicitly defined as the nearest preceding section-only heading, a
  line qualifies only when its visual row has no values in any data column.
  Assign headings only after all data rows have been enumerated. A data-bearing
  label must retain the preceding qualifying heading and must not replace it.
- This value-less restriction is specific to section-only heading fields.
  A field defined by a governing geography or group heading follows that
  schema-defined role: an excluded heading can govern detail rows even when it
  contains numeric group aggregates. Do not emit that heading as a detail row.
- Use the prior source context to resolve a heading omitted at the current
  page's start, within the same source table and block. A heading printed at
  the preceding page's end governs following details even if no detail rows
  followed it there. Do not carry the last emitted row's label in its place.
  Scan backward past detail rows in the prior excerpt to find the nearest
  heading of the category required by the field. It need not be the final
  source line. A heading in that supplied source is printed evidence; do not
  leave the field null merely because it is absent from the current image.
  Current-page headings supersede prior headings in source order; never apply
  one heading to an entire page containing several different blocks.
  If a required heading cannot be resolved, retain eligible detail rows with
  that heading field null rather than omitting the rows.
- Apply the active qualifying heading to every following data row until another
  qualifying heading appears. When a field description names a heading category,
  use only the printed line or block that satisfies that category. Do not append
  an adjacent title, subtitle, description, or document label.
- Before returning, audit every string-valued cell against its field
  description. Removing whitespace inside a source delimiter never removes the
  delimiter itself. Preserve all source delimiters unless that field description
  explicitly says to remove them. Whitespace-only normalization inside a
  delimiter pair changes `( value )` to `(value)`, never to `value`.
- For section-only heading fields, before returning, audit every carried heading
  field. If its value is copied from a row that has any data-column value, it is
  not a qualifying heading;
  restore the nearest preceding value-less heading, or emit null on a
  continuation page so the ordered parser can carry it.
- For a category-qualified heading, stop before an adjacent line whose role is
  a title, subtitle, content-type label, or description rather than part of that
  category heading.

Continuation rules:
- Re-audit the table identity using actual printed titles, identifiers, and the
  supplied prior source context. A changed whole-table identifier starts a new
  record with the appropriate schema unit, even if a supplied flag says otherwise.
  A forward-looking continues footer is not evidence of incoming continuation.
  This includes "Continues" appended to a printed table title in the footer;
  compare table numbers before interpreting such a suffix.
- Audit the table continuation evidence against any current-of-total marker in
  the page text. For a marker belonging to the table, current 1 means a new
  table page and current greater than 1 means a continuation. Use the marker
  when a supplied continuation flag contradicts it.
- A table marked as a continuation after that audit is not a new schema unit,
  whether or not headers repeat.
- For a new table or source block, set unit_name to the exact array unit name
  from the schema units supplied above and set is_continuation to false.
- For a continuation, set is_continuation to true. If the owning unit is not
  explicitly identifiable from the printed table identity and supplied source
  context, set unit_name to null; the ordered
  parser will attach it to the preceding record. Do not classify continuation
  rows by their subject matter.
- Leave an aggregation-policy carry field null when its value is not printed on
  this continuation page. The ordered parser carries the preceding value.
- For aggregation-policy sequence fields, emit a page-local 1-based ordinal.
  The ordered parser assigns the document-wide sequence.
- rows contains only values visible on this page and must match the selected
  unit's item schema. Do not emit rows from another page. Prior-page context
  supplies only block identity and governing labels, never additional detail rows.
- source.page_index is the current zero-based page index. source.table_index is
  the zero-based index in the supplied page-tables array, or null for a
  non-table block.
- Never invent a continuation, unit name, or value.
- Check document_fields against their own requested scope. A regional subtotal
  cannot supply a requested national total. Leave unsupported scalars null.
- Finally audit the first eligible row on the current page, including any
  rows before the first printed heading. Their labels may come from prior source
  context, but their detail values must come from this page.

